WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.020
Speaker 1: In his latest orange rant, the
failed former and wannabe future president

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.360
Donald Trump is promising mandatory homeless
camps.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.360
He looks completely disoriented.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.200
He looks swollen as usual, his right eye almost
completely shut.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.680
But he says that he will ban homelessness.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.070
Hey, who knew homelessness had such a simple
solution?

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.070
Just ban it.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.939
By the way, Ban the virus while you're at
it, let's get rid of everything.

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:32.090
And he says that there will be tent camps
for the homeless outside of cities.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.430
This is so deranged.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:39.809
We heard him talk about it at rallies, but
it is now being put out as an actual idea

00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:43.590
of of policy, if you can call it policy for
Trump's 2024 run.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:47.469
Let's take a look at this, then I'm going
to talk to you about the ugly dystopian history

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:48.800
of camps for people.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.340
Speaker 2: Ban urban camping.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.200
Wherever possible.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.260
Violators of these bans will be arrested,
but they will be given the option to accept

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:02.300
treatment and services if they're willing
to be rehabilitated.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.019
Many of them don't want that.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:06.390
We'll give them the option.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:12.750
We will then open up large parcels of inexpensive
land, bring in doctors, psychiatrists, social

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:19.710
workers and drug rehab specialists, and create
tent cities where the homeless can be relocated

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.640
and their problems identified.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.740
But we'll open up our cities again, make them
livable and make them beautiful.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:27.740
Yeah.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:28.740
Ben Urban can't.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.439
Speaker 1: Cillessen pretending to take this
seriously for a second.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:41.720
I'm not big on tent cities and there is a
lot of historical precedent for these sorts

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.460
of camps being some of the ugliest things
that societies do.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:51.960
I mean, not only is it inhumane and violates
basic human rights, it also doesn't work.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.320
And I always think it's important when we
talk about these things, to talk to those

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.460
who might support such a policy, not only
ideologically, but also pragmatically.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:06.640
So the first problem is that banning homelessness
in this way, it assumes that homelessness

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.490
is a choice or a behavior that you can eliminate
with the right punishment.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.640
And that's a flawed assumption at its core.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.989
It ignores that homelessness has so many complex
factors that causes it.

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:21.680
Poverty is, of course, part of it.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.610
It's simple to say, well, poor homeless people
are poor.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.300
Poor people are more likely to become homeless.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.060
Yes, that's absolutely true.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.070
Mental illness is a component.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:33.040
Addiction is a component.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.610
Lack of affordable housing in cities is a
component.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:42.349
Lack of effective public transportation that
can link lower income residential areas with

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:44.730
areas where the jobs are is a factor.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:45.730
I could go on.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:46.760
There's so many different factors.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.900
But what the takeaway should be is if you
just criminalize homelessness, it doesn't

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.750
really solve anything.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.819
So when you try to ban homelessness and you
try to force homeless people to live in a

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:04.860
tent camp outside of a city where I guess
they would be bused to it, once you're there,

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:05.860
there's no jobs.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.560
You're in the middle of nowhere and there's
a tent camp and that's it.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.560
You're doing nothing to address the underlying
conditions that got people to be homeless

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.560
in the first place.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.430
But what you do is you further marginalize
them.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.239
You stigmatize the homeless population because
you're actually separating them as they go.

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:25.750
If you're homeless, you're being brought out
there to the camp where you have no shot at

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:26.750
getting a job.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:32.090
I mean, forget about it and you actually perpetuate
the problem and make it even worse.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.670
Now, if you want to look at some historical
examples of this type of approach to homelessness,

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:42.560
there are many historical failures in the
20th century.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.470
Many countries implemented mandatory institutionalization
programs for homeless people.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:51.700
And the idea was we're going to provide shelter
and treatment, which is arguably a good a

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:52.700
nice thing, right?

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.120
I mean, when Trump says we're going to get
people things that they need, we'll get them

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.470
shelter, we'll get them food, we'll get them
treatment, at least on an interim basis, once

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:02.390
we've completely separated them from society.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:09.450
We know from the 20th century examples that
they end up being overcrowded, unsanitary,

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:18.199
abusive, very little effective treatment provided,
stigmatizing and alienating and segregating

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:20.790
so it doesn't actually accomplish anything.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:26.500
And what you end up with is with many of these
homeless folks essentially trapped in these

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:33.460
places for years with conditions they may
have worsening chances of reintegrating into

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.220
society, dramatically decreasing.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:40.250
And meanwhile, the homeless population continues
to swell.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:47.080
Another example of this is looking specifically
at tent cities to house homeless populations.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.000
In the early 2000s, there were cities in the
Pacific Northwest that established some tent

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.210
cities for homeless people.

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.990
And the idea is temporary shelter.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:57.090
Temporary shelter.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:00.380
And exactly what I just described to you is,
is what took place.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:03.389
They became overcrowded, they became unsanitary.

00:05:03.389 --> 00:05:05.250
They weren't even properly funded.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.530
There was limited access to basic amenities.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:13.120
Running water was scarce, Sanitation facilities
were leaving much to be desired.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.650
And they actually became hotspots for crime
and drug abuse, which only made the problem

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:19.650
worse.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:20.930
Marginalized people stigmatized them.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.800
And you're not making it more likely that
there will be what Republicans like to describe

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.990
as productive members of society.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:35.039
So instead of punishing with further marginalizing
programs for a population that's already vulnerable,

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:39.710
we should instead be working towards what
causes the homelessness.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:40.710
What?

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.400
Imagine you say, Hey, look.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.610
All these homeless people.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.750
Let's not even think about what made them
homeless.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:52.740
Let's just ship them off in bus busses to
the desert and have them live in tents indefinitely.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.000
Until what?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.370
Until what happens?

00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:01.410
So poverty, lack of affordable housing, lack
of support for people in their communities

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.139
rather than by separating them.

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:07.130
These are the types of things that will help
people reintegrate into society.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:12.009
And many of these programs that are proposed
and often underfunded, they're actually quite

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:14.889
revenue neutral because, yes, they cost money.

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:21.500
But if you care mostly about the finances,
they eventually generate government revenue

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:27.540
by getting some fraction of those people back
to work, paying taxes, spending money in the

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:28.540
community.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:31.229
So there's even a fiscal conservatism argument
to be made.

00:06:31.229 --> 00:06:34.669
Swollen Trump, though I don't think he's ready
to have this conversation

